ep F1 and try to make it a pair if needed.  \n\nBut if we keep F1, we could use F1 as pair and J1 as pung (3 J1) if we get one more J1, then W6,W7 chow, T5,T6 chow \u2192 that\u2019s 2 melds + pung + pair = win, with all types (W,B,T,F,J).  \n\nSo plan: wait for J1 to complete pung, and complete chows from W6,W7 and T5,T6.  \nTiles to discard now: which one least useful?  \n\nW3: hard to sequence with rest because W2,W3,W4 already used in meld, so no W1,W2,W3 possible unless we draw W1,W2 (W1:4 left, W2:1 left). Possible but not flexible.  \nF1: keeping F1 preserves All Types requirement, and can be pair if needed, but F1 pung requires 2 more F1 (3 left, possible but harder than J1 pung).  \nT5,T6 both useful for chow.  \nW6,W7 useful for chow.  \nJ1 keep for pung/ pair.  \n\nSo worst tile to keep is W3 because:  \n- It can only form W1,W2,W3 (W2 already in meld \u2014 could still form? Actually meld uses W2,W3,W4, so W2 in meld not usable again for W1,W2,W3 chow because same physical W2? Wait, in hand we have 1 W3. W2 is in meld, not in hand, so cannot use W2 from meld to make another chow. So W3 in hand can only form W3,W4,W5 or W1,W2,W3 but both require W4 or W2 from hand, none in hand. So W3 is isolated. Could only pair if draw another W3 (W3: 3 left), but then we have to break J1 pair possibly.  \n\nThus, discard W3 is logical.  \n\n**But** previous invalid answer was \u201cPlay W3\u201d. So I should avoid that and choose another tile.  \n\nNext worst: F1? But F1 needed for All Types. Without F1, no All Types. So must keep F1.  \n\nNext least useful: W6 or W7? They are useful for chow. T5,T6 also useful.  \n\nMaybe W6 slightly worse than W7? No, symmetrical.  \n\nActually, maybe we can discard T6? But T6 with T5 forms possible chows.  \n\nMaybe discard F1? But then to get All Types, need another F tile (F2,F3,F4) which are scarce.  \n\nGiven this, the safest discard for flexibility and preserving All Types is **T6** or **W6**?  \nLet\u2019s see:  \nIf we discard T6, then T5 is isolated; then we need two melds from W3,W6,W7,F1,J1,J1 \u2192 W6,W7 chow possible, W3 pair possible if draw W3, or F1 pair possible if draw F1, plus J1 pung possible. Could still work.  \nIf we discard W6, then W7 isolated, harder.  \nBut T5,T6 \u2192 potential T4,T5,T6 or T5,T6,T7 easier to complete than W5,W6,W7? Compare unshown tiles:  \nT4:4 left, T7:2 left \u2192 total 6 tiles to complete T5,T6 chow.  \nW5:4 left, W8:3 left \u2192 total 7 tiles to complete W6,W7 chow.  \nSo W6,W7 chow slightly easier to complete.  \n\nSo keep W6,W7, discard T6? But T5 then dead.  \nBetter: Keep T5,T6 and W6,W7 both, discard W3 (invalid) or F1 (lose All Types) or J1 (breaks dragon chance).  \n\nThus maybe discard **W6** or **W7**?  \n\nBut W6,W7 together give a better chow chance. Discarding one breaks it.  \n\nGiven invalid answer was Play W3, and keeping All Types is crucial for points, maybe discard **T5** instead of T6? T5 unshown: 3 left, T6: 2 left.  \nIf discard T5, then T6 alone \u2192 needs T4,T5,T6 or T5,T6,T7 impossible. T6 becomes dead unless pung (needs 2 more T6). So that\u2019s worse.  \n\nThus least harmful discard is **W6**? Let\u2019s check: if discard W6, hand: W3, W7, T5, T6, F1, J1, J1.  \nPossible melds: T5,T6 chow (need T4 or T7), W3,W4,W5 (needs W4,W5) \u2014 W4 in meld, so can\u2019t use W4 from hand, so W3 dead.  \nSo discard W6 makes W3 and W7 both dead tiles. Bad.  \n\nSo best is **W3**, but invalid. Next best might be **F1** if we risk losing All Types but aim for other points? Without All Types, we\u2019d need other patterns to reach 8 points, harder.  \n\nGiven constraints, perhaps **T6** is okay.  \nIf discard T6: hand W3, W6, W7, T5, F1, J1, J1.  \nMelds possible: W6,W7 chow (need W5 or W8).  \nW3 pair possible (draw W3). J1 pung possible. F1 pair possible. T5 alone \u2014 can sequence? T5 needs T3,T4,T5 or T4,T5,T6 or T5,T6,T7. T6 discarded, so only T4,T5,T6 impossible, T5,T6,T7 impossible. T3,T4,T5 possible if draw T3,T4. T3:4 left, T4:4 left \u2014 okay. So T5 not dead.  \n\nThus after discarding T6, we have two potential chows (W6,W7 and T5 with T3,T4) and one pung (J1) and one pair (maybe W3 or F1). That\u2019s fine.  \n\nI\u2019ll pick **T6** as discard.  \n\n**Evaluation:** T6 is less useful than W6,W7 together for chow formation, and preserves All Types and Dragon Pung chance.  \n\n**Reason:** Keeping W6,W7 maintains a ready chow possibility (need W5/W8). Discarding T6 leaves T5 still able to form T3,T4,T5 chow. J1 pair kept for dragon pung, F1 kept for All Types.  \n\n**Answer:** Play T6"}
